const articles = [
  {
    tag:"Strategy",title:"Why Your Thumbnails Aren't Getting Clicks",meta:"By Victor Vandi · 6 min read · Thumbnail Strategy",
    body:`<p>Here's the uncomfortable truth: most creators approach thumbnail design completely backwards. They ask, <em>"Does this look good?"</em> when they should be asking, <em>"Does this make someone feel something in under half a second?"</em></p><p>I've looked at thousands of thumbnails across hundreds of niches. The ones with low CTR almost always share the same core problem — they communicate <strong>too much, too quietly</strong>. They're visually polite in a feed that rewards visual aggression.</p><h3>The Feed Is a War</h3><p>When a viewer opens YouTube, they see between 6 and 20 thumbnails at once. Your thumbnail competes against all of them simultaneously. Every single one is screaming for attention. If yours whispers, it loses.</p><p>Low CTR is almost never a quality problem. It's a hierarchy problem. There's no single dominant element grabbing the eye. The face, the text, the background — they're all fighting each other at equal volume.</p><div class="highlight">"Your thumbnail should have one job: cause curiosity or promise a reward. Everything else is noise."</div><h3>The 3 Most Common Mistakes</h3><p><strong>1. Too many elements.</strong> A face, a logo, a background scene, text, and an arrow. Each new element dilutes the power of every other element. Top-performing thumbnails are brutally simple.</p><p><strong>2. Low contrast between subject and background.</strong> If your face blends into the background, the viewer's eye slides past you. The foreground subject needs to pop — through lighting, color opposition, or a hard edge created in post.</p><p><strong>3. Text that describes instead of provokes.</strong> "My Morning Routine" describes. "I Woke Up at 4AM for 30 Days" provokes. One tells you what the video is. The other makes you need to know what happened.</p><h3>The Fix: One Question</h3><p>Before you finalize any thumbnail, ask: <strong>If I had never heard of this channel, would I click this?</strong> If the answer is "probably not," you know what to do. Simplify, amplify, and provoke. CTR is a byproduct of emotion.</p>`
  },
  {
    tag:"Psychology",title:"The Science of the First 0.4 Seconds",meta:"By Victor Vandi · 7 min read · Viewer Psychology",
    body:`<p>Eye-tracking research on digital interfaces consistently shows the same result: the average person spends between 0.3 and 0.5 seconds evaluating a visual element before deciding whether to engage with it. On a busy YouTube feed, that number is probably even lower.</p><p>This means your thumbnail is not being read. It's being felt. The conscious decision to click comes after — and it's largely a rationalization of an emotional reaction that already happened.</p><h3>What the Brain Actually Processes First</h3><p>In those first fractions of a second, the visual cortex picks up on three things before anything else: <strong>high contrast edges</strong>, <strong>human faces</strong>, and <strong>color temperature shifts</strong>. Text, context, and meaning come after — usually by a full second or more.</p><p>This is why a thumbnail with a sharp-lit face against a dark background triggers a response before a viewer even knows what the video is about. The brain says "human, important" and the click follows.</p><div class="highlight">"By the time a viewer reads your thumbnail text, they've already decided whether they're interested. The text just confirms the decision."</div><h3>Pattern Interruption</h3><p>The human brain is a pattern-matching machine. When it sees the same type of visual input repeatedly, it stops paying attention — habituation. In a feed full of bright thumbnails with smiling faces, a stark, dark, cinematic frame is a pattern interrupt. The brain notices it precisely because it's different.</p><p>This is why copying what big channels do is a losing strategy. By the time a style is widespread, viewers are habituated to it. You need to interrupt the pattern of your specific feed, not follow it.</p><h3>The Curiosity Gap</h3><p>Psychologist George Loewenstein's research on curiosity shows that people experience genuine discomfort when aware of a gap between what they know and what they want to know. A good thumbnail deliberately creates that gap. Design for that gap. Show enough to provoke curiosity. Withhold enough to leave it open.</p>`
  },
  {
    tag:"Design",title:"Color Contrast: The Unfair Advantage",meta:"By Victor Vandi · 5 min read · Design Fundamentals",
    body:`<p>I can predict within about 80% accuracy which thumbnails in a competitive niche will outperform the others — before looking at a single data point — just by evaluating contrast. It's that reliable.</p><p>Contrast isn't one thing. It's three: <strong>value contrast</strong> (light vs dark), <strong>color contrast</strong> (warm vs cool), and <strong>edge contrast</strong> (hard edges separating elements). All three matter. Most thumbnails get at most one right.</p><h3>Value Contrast: The Foundation</h3><p>The eye follows the lightest element in a frame first. This isn't a creative opinion — it's how human vision is wired. Bright things in dark environments meant fire, or sun, or a threat worth noticing.</p><p>If you want the viewer's eye to land on your face first, your face should be the brightest element in the frame. If you want text to dominate, the text should sit on the highest-contrast portion of the background.</p><div class="highlight">"Put a white subject on a black background and you have the most powerful attention-grabbing combination in visual design. It's worked for 10,000 years. It still works."</div><h3>Color Opposition</h3><p>Complementary colors — those opposite each other on the color wheel — create vibration at their boundary. Orange and blue. Red and teal. Yellow and purple. This vibration is physiologically stimulating. The most clicked thumbnails almost always feature opposing color relationships. You don't need to be sophisticated. Pick one warm element and one cool element, both saturated, in proximity. That's enough.</p><h3>Edge Contrast and Subject Separation</h3><p>The third piece is clean subject separation. If your main subject doesn't have a clear edge separating it from the background, the eye gets confused. In Photoshop: clean selection, slight brightening of the subject, subtle edge light on the subject side. Five minutes on subject separation is worth an hour on everything else.</p>`
  },
  {
    tag:"Typography",title:"Thumbnail Text Rules That Pros Never Break",meta:"By Victor Vandi · 6 min read · Typography",
    body:`<p>Text on a thumbnail has a completely different job than text anywhere else. It's not there to be read from start to finish. It's there to be absorbed in a peripheral glance and trigger a gut reaction. Once you internalize that, everything about thumbnail typography becomes clear.</p><h3>Rule 1: Five Words Maximum</h3><p>If your thumbnail text requires more than five words, you're writing a sentence when you should be writing a trigger. Long text signals that the creator doesn't trust the image to do the work. The best thumbnail text averages 2 to 4 words: "I Was Wrong." "They Lied To Us." "The $0 Method."</p><h3>Rule 2: One Hierarchy Level</h3><p>Pick one piece of text to dominate. Make it enormous. Make everything else either absent or clearly subordinate. Two equally-sized text blocks fight each other. Indecision at the design level creates indecision at the click level.</p><div class="highlight">"The best thumbnails I've ever made had the least text. That's not a coincidence."</div><h3>Rule 3: Contrast Between Text and Background</h3><p>White text needs a dark region behind it. Dark text needs a light region. If you're placing text over a complex background, you need a treatment — a gradient overlay, blur behind the text, or a semi-transparent block. Test readability at thumbnail size on a phone screen.</p><h3>Rule 4: Use Display Fonts, Not Body Fonts</h3><p>Thin or serifed fonts fail at thumbnail size. Bold condensed display fonts — Impact, Bebas Neue, Anton — dominate thumbnails because they carry weight at small sizes. They were designed to be read at a distance. Use them.</p><h3>Rule 5: Text Must Add What the Image Doesn't</h3><p>If your thumbnail shows a shocked face and your text says "I Was Shocked," the text is redundant. Text should <em>extend</em> the image. The image shows the emotion; the text hints at the cause. Together they create the full curiosity gap.</p>`
  },
  {
    tag:"Psychology",title:"How to Use Facial Expressions to Force Clicks",meta:"By Victor Vandi · 7 min read · Human Psychology",
    body:`<p>The human face is the most click-generating element in YouTube thumbnails — by a significant margin. This isn't a design opinion. It's documented in YouTube's own creator research and confirmed by independent CTR studies across thousands of videos.</p><p>But here's the problem: most creators slap a neutral or mildly smiling face on a thumbnail and wonder why it doesn't perform. The face alone isn't enough. The <em>expression</em> is everything.</p><h3>The Neuroscience of Mirror Neurons</h3><p>Humans have mirror neurons — brain cells that fire when we observe someone else experiencing an emotion, as if we were experiencing it ourselves. On a thumbnail, an intense facial expression triggers a micro-activation of the same emotion in the viewer. A viewer who has a micro-experience of an emotion wants to understand what caused it. That's a click.</p><div class="highlight">"Neutral faces don't get clicks. Emotionally extreme faces do. The bigger the expression, the stronger the mirror neuron response."</div><h3>Which Expressions Perform Best</h3><p><strong>Shock/Disbelief:</strong> Wide eyes, open mouth, raised eyebrows. Consistently the highest-performing expression across niches. Triggers curiosity about what caused the reaction.</p><p><strong>Disgust/Disapproval:</strong> Works well in opinion content, reactions, and controversy. Viewers want to know what provoked such a strong negative reaction.</p><p><strong>Intense Focus/Determination:</strong> Works in fitness, finance, and self-improvement. Communicates seriousness and stakes.</p><p><strong>Genuine Laughter:</strong> Works in entertainment content. Signals reward. Only works if it looks genuinely unposed.</p><h3>The Direction of the Gaze</h3><p>Eyes looking directly at camera create direct connection and feel personal. Eyes looking off to one side create intrigue. Eyes looking at on-screen text direct the viewer to read that text. This is a small detail that compounds at scale — test it.</p>`
  },
  {
    tag:"Composition",title:"The Rule of Thirds Is Killing Your CTR",meta:"By Victor Vandi · 6 min read · Visual Composition",
    body:`<p>The rule of thirds is one of the most useful composition guidelines in photography. It's almost completely wrong for YouTube thumbnails.</p><p>The rule says to place your subject on one of the four intersections of a 3x3 grid, leaving negative space to create visual tension. Beautiful in a landscape photograph. Damaging in a 16:9 thumbnail competing against 15 other images in a viewer's peripheral vision.</p><h3>Why Thumbnails Are Different</h3><p>In a still photograph, you have the viewer's full attention. In a YouTube feed, you have a fraction of a second of peripheral vision. The negative space that creates elegance in photography creates emptiness in a thumbnail. The off-center placement that feels dynamic in a portrait feels weak in a tiny grid item.</p><div class="highlight">"Photography rewards subtlety. Thumbnails reward clarity. The same design instincts that serve one will betray the other."</div><h3>What Actually Works</h3><p><strong>Fill the frame.</strong> Your subject should occupy as much of the frame as the content allows. Tight crops on faces dramatically outperform wide shots in almost every test. The face should feel too close, like it's breaking through the screen.</p><p><strong>Create left-right tension.</strong> Place your main visual element on one side and your text on the other. The eye enters the frame, reads one element, then moves to the other.</p><p><strong>Use the bottom third for text.</strong> It's the last place the eye rests before making a click decision. Text placed here acts as a closer — it provides the final piece of information that converts interest into action.</p><h3>The Baseline Formula</h3><p>Large face on the left third to half of the frame, direct gaze, bold text on the right or bottom, high contrast between subject and background, maximum 4 words. Use this as a baseline and deviate intentionally — not because you're following photography rules that don't apply here.</p>`
  },
  {
    tag:"Branding",title:"Build a Thumbnail Brand in 3 Decisions",meta:"By Victor Vandi · 5 min read · Channel Branding",
    body:`<p>There's a moment every growing creator reaches when viewers start recognizing their thumbnails before they read the channel name. That moment doesn't happen by accident. It's the result of consistent visual decisions repeated across dozens or hundreds of videos.</p><p>MrBeast didn't become visually recognizable by accident. Neither did MKBHD, Mark Rober, or Veritasium. Each made a small number of consistent visual decisions and refused to deviate. That consistency compounds into brand recognition — essentially free CTR from returning viewers.</p><h3>Decision 1: Your Color Signature</h3><p>Pick one or two colors that will appear in every thumbnail. Not because they're beautiful — because they're yours. MKBHD uses deep blacks with precise product photography. When you've seen enough of their thumbnails, you recognize the visual DNA before you register anything else. Once chosen, protect your colors. Don't deviate for trends.</p><div class="highlight">"Brand recognition is compounding interest on visual consistency. Every thumbnail you publish at the same visual standard makes the next one slightly more valuable."</div><h3>Decision 2: Your Typography System</h3><p>Choose one display font for headlines and use it on every thumbnail. One. Returning viewers will start to recognize your text style as part of your identity — even without consciously noticing it. Your font should match your tone: bold condensed for urgency, slab serif for authority, geometric sans for precision. Choose intentionally, then commit.</p><h3>Decision 3: Your Framing Signature</h3><p>The way you crop, frame, and light your subject should be consistent. Pick a direction authentic to your content and execute it the same way every time. When a new viewer sees five of your thumbnails in a grid, they should feel like they came from the same creative mind. That coherence builds trust and makes your channel shelf feel designed rather than random.</p>`
  },
  {
    tag:"Testing",title:"How to Test Thumbnails and Actually Learn",meta:"By Victor Vandi · 8 min read · Data & Testing",
    body:`<p>YouTube's Test & Compare feature is one of the most powerful tools available to creators. Most creators use it exactly once, see a result they half-expected, and file it away as confirmed. That's not testing. That's confirmation bias with extra steps.</p><p>Real thumbnail testing is a system, not an event.</p><h3>Test One Variable at a Time</h3><p>If you change the face, the text, and the background color between two thumbnails, you learn which version won — but not why. That knowledge doesn't transfer to your next video. Good isolated tests: face vs no face, text on left vs text on right, warm vs cool color grade, tight crop vs medium shot. Each test should teach you something that applies to every future thumbnail.</p><div class="highlight">"The goal of testing isn't to find the winning thumbnail for this video. It's to build a model of what works for your specific audience."</div><h3>Sample Size and Patience</h3><p>A test run on 200 impressions is noise. A test run on 10,000 impressions is data. Let tests run for a minimum of 5 to 7 days before drawing conclusions. Early results often reverse as the initial audience gives way to cold traffic, which is a different viewer entirely.</p><h3>Beyond CTR: The Metric That Actually Matters</h3><p>CTR tells you how many people clicked. Average View Duration and Watch Time per impression tell you whether those clicks were valuable. A thumbnail with higher CTR but 40% lower AVD is probably clickbait. The ideal thumbnail maximizes <em>qualified</em> clicks — people who want exactly what your video delivers.</p><h3>Keep a Testing Log</h3><p>For every test: date, video topic, variable tested, winner, margin, and your hypothesis going in vs what you observed. After 20 or 30 tests, patterns will emerge specific to your channel, audience, and niche. That pattern library is worth more than any generic thumbnail advice — including this article.</p>`
  },
  {
    tag:"Niches",title:"Thumbnail Rules Are Different for Every Niche",meta:"By Victor Vandi · 8 min read · Niche Strategy",
    body:`<p>One of the most common mistakes from growing creators is applying thumbnail strategies from one niche to another. What generates 12% CTR in finance produces 3% in gaming. The audiences are different, the visual vocabulary is different, and the emotional triggers are completely different.</p><h3>Finance & Business</h3><p>Finance thumbnails are driven by numbers and stakes. Large, bold numbers create instant curiosity — "Is that real? How?" Faces should look serious or visibly surprised, never casual or smiling (which reads as untrustworthy in this niche). Controlled color palettes, dark backgrounds, gold or green accents. Luxury signals matter. Sloppiness kills CTR.</p><h3>Gaming</h3><p>Gaming is the niche with the most visual noise. The way to win is either out-loud everyone (high skill required) or use dramatic minimalism that interrupts the feed. Character faces in extreme closeup consistently outperform game-scene screenshots. Stakes must feel personal — not just "hard boss" but "I almost quit."</p><div class="highlight">"Niche fluency is knowing what emotional frequency your specific audience is tuned to — and broadcasting on exactly that frequency."</div><h3>Tech & Reviews</h3><p>Tech audiences are highly skeptical. Thumbnails that feel honest — clean, uncluttered, product-forward — earn more trust than dramatic emotional faces. The key emotional lever is informed authority: this person knows more than I do. Product shots should be immaculate. Background clean or dark.</p><h3>Health & Fitness</h3><p>Transformation is the central promise of fitness content. Before/after splits work because they promise an answer to the viewer's most important question: does this actually work? Avoid the "gym mirror selfie" aesthetic — it reads as vanity rather than value.</p><h3>Education & Explainers</h3><p>Educational thumbnails need to communicate "I will explain something complicated in a way that's actually understandable." The face should look engaged and slightly conspiratorial — like you're sharing a secret. Avoid academic aesthetics, which signal effort and work rather than accessible revelation.</p>`
  },
  {
    tag:"Advanced",title:"The Cinematic Thumbnail Formula I Use on Every Project",meta:"By Victor Vandi · 9 min read · Professional Technique",
    body:`<p>After designing thumbnails for over 300 videos across dozens of niches, I've distilled my process into a framework I can execute consistently and reliably. This is not vague principle. It's the actual step-by-step process I follow on every project.</p><h3>Step 1: Identify the Single Emotional Promise</h3><p>Before I open Photoshop, I ask one question: what is the one thing this thumbnail needs to make the viewer feel? Not think. Feel. Curiosity, surprise, urgency, aspiration, fear of missing out — pick one. Every design decision that follows serves that single emotional promise. If a design element doesn't reinforce that emotion, it doesn't belong.</p><h3>Step 2: Choose the Dominant Element</h3><p>One element leads. Everything else supports. The dominant element is usually a face, but could be a number, a bold text phrase, or an object with strong visual weight. It will occupy the majority of the visual real estate and will be the highest-contrast element in the frame. I decide this before touching a single layer.</p><div class="highlight">"Professional thumbnail design is mostly elimination. You start with everything and remove anything that isn't essential."</div><h3>Step 3: Build the Background First</h3><p>I work background-first. The background sets the emotional tone, color temperature, and contrast framework into which everything else is placed. A good background establishes mood, creates contrast with the foreground subject, and has no detail where text will sit. My typical background: dark gradient, subtle radial light source behind where the subject will stand, light grain overlay at ~3% for cinematic texture.</p><h3>Step 4: Subject Extraction and Lighting</h3><p>Clean extraction with feathered edges, a shadow added at ground level, and a rim light or edge highlight in Photoshop to match the imagined light source in the background. Five to ten minutes on this step transforms a pasted cutout into a composited scene.</p><h3>Step 5: Color Grade Everything Together</h3><p>Unified color grade using a Curves adjustment layer (slight S-curve), Color Balance layer (push shadows toward the accent color slightly), and Hue/Saturation layer (desaturate everything except the dominant color). This step makes the thumbnail feel like a film still rather than a graphic design.</p><h3>Step 6: The Small-Screen Test</h3><p>Export at full size, then reduce to 100px wide — roughly what it looks like on a phone feed. The dominant element should still be immediately readable, the contrast should hold, and the emotional tone should still be clear. If any of those fail at small size, amplify. No exceptions.</p>`
  },
  {
    tag:"Metric",title:"Click-Through Rate: The Only Number That Honestly Judges Your Thumbnail",meta:"By Victor Vandi · 5 min read · Fundamentals",
    body:`<p>Views are vanity. Watch time is aspirational. But <strong>Click-Through Rate (CTR)</strong> is brutal, honest feedback from a cold audience that owes you nothing. It tells you one simple thing: did your thumbnail and title convince a stranger to choose your video over everything else on their screen?</p><p>YouTube defines CTR as the percentage of people who clicked your video after seeing its thumbnail in a feed, search result, or recommendation. If 100 people see your thumbnail and 5 click it, your CTR is 5%. The platform average sits between 2–10%, but strong channels in competitive niches regularly hit 8–15%.</p><div class="highlight">"Your thumbnail is a billboard on the world's busiest highway. CTR tells you if anyone is turning their head."</div><p>Here's what most creators miss: YouTube uses CTR as a <strong>distribution signal</strong>. A higher CTR tells the algorithm your content is relevant and compelling to the audience it's being shown to, which rewards you with more impressions. More impressions with continued high CTR creates an upward spiral.</p><p>But CTR alone is misleading. A clickbait thumbnail can spike CTR while destroying your Average View Duration (AVD). YouTube watches both. The goal is a <strong>high CTR with sustained watch time</strong> — that combination is what triggers mass distribution.</p>`
  },
  {
    tag:"Framework",title:"My Strategy for Thumbnails: A Repeatable System That Removes Guesswork",meta:"By Victor Vandi · 6 min read · Fundamentals",
    body:`<p>Most creators design thumbnails by feel — they open Canva, pick something that looks good, and hope. That's not strategy. Strategy means you know <em>before</em> you open any tool what your thumbnail needs to do, say, and show. It means you have a repeatable process, not a random creative moment.</p><p>A solid thumbnail strategy starts with the <strong>promise</strong>. Every thumbnail makes a silent promise to the viewer: "Click here and you will get this." Define that promise first. Then reverse-engineer the visual that communicates it fastest. The visual doesn't have to be beautiful — it has to be <strong>clear, emotionally resonant, and specific.</strong></p><p>The strategy also means thinking about <strong>audience state</strong>. Is your viewer on a phone during a commute? Bored at 2am? Actively searching for a solution? The emotional state they're in when they see your thumbnail changes what visual cues will hook them.</p><div class="highlight">"Strategy is deciding what NOT to include as much as what to include."</div><p>Finally, your strategy must account for <strong>your channel's stage</strong>. Early channels need to steal attention fast — bold, simple, high-contrast thumbnails work best. Established channels can afford subtler branding because their audience already recognises them.</p>`
  },
  {
    tag:"Design",title:"Elements: The Building Blocks Every YouTube Thumbnail Is Made Of",meta:"By Victor Vandi · 5 min read · Strategy",
    body:`<p>A YouTube thumbnail is not one thing — it's a <strong>combination of visual elements</strong> working together to communicate a message in under two seconds. Understanding what those elements are gives you control over what you're creating instead of just improvising.</p><p>The core elements are: <strong>Subject</strong> (usually a face, person, or object — the focal point), <strong>Background</strong> (the context or atmosphere behind the subject), <strong>Text</strong> (optional but often critical — usually 3–5 words max), <strong>Colour blocks or graphic shapes</strong> (used to create contrast or direct attention), and <strong>Facial expression or emotion</strong> (the most psychologically powerful element when a human is featured).</p><p>Each element competes for the viewer's attention. The mistake most creators make is adding elements without asking: <strong>"Is this earning its space?"</strong> Every element either helps the viewer understand the promise faster, or it slows them down. There is no neutral element — if it's not helping, it's hurting.</p><div class="highlight">"Element hierarchy: Subject → Emotion/Expression → Text → Supporting Graphics. The viewer's eye should follow this path naturally."</div><p>The most underrated element is <strong>empty space</strong>. White space (or dark space) gives the subject room to breathe and makes the thumbnail feel intentional rather than cluttered. Busy thumbnails signal amateur work, even if each individual element is well-designed.</p>`
  },
  {
    tag:"Visuals",title:"Still Image: Why the Frame You Choose Can Win or Lose the Click",meta:"By Victor Vandi · 5 min read · Strategy",
    body:`<p>Many creators use an auto-generated still from their video as the thumbnail. This is almost always a mistake. YouTube's auto-stills are pulled at random timestamps — you might get a blinking frame, a blurred transition, or an unflattering mid-sentence expression. These frames were never composed with a thumbnail in mind.</p><p>A <strong>purposefully composed still image</strong> is different. It's either a custom photo taken specifically for the thumbnail, or a hand-picked frame from your footage chosen because it communicates the emotional peak of the video. The difference in click performance can be significant.</p><div class="highlight">"The best thumbnail still captures the emotional climax of your content — the moment that makes someone think 'I need to see how this happened.'"</div><p>When choosing a still, look for: <strong>sharp focus on the subject, strong expression if a face is involved, natural framing that leaves room for text, and a background that doesn't fight for attention.</strong></p><p>If you're doing video editing, build the habit of identifying your <strong>thumbnail moment</strong> during the edit — the exact frame that tells the story of the whole video. Export it at full resolution, then use it as your base.</p>`
  },
  {
    tag:"Principle",title:"The Less the Better: Why Thumbnail Simplicity Beats Visual Complexity Every Time",meta:"By Victor Vandi · 5 min read · Principles",
    body:`<p>There's a temptation — especially for beginners — to fill a thumbnail with everything that's exciting about the video. Multiple faces, a long title, arrows, borders, icons, colour gradients. The result is a thumbnail that looks like it's trying too hard, because it is.</p><p>YouTube thumbnails are displayed at small sizes in fast-scrolling feeds. At 168×94 pixels (the standard browse feed size), a complex design becomes noise. Your viewer doesn't slow down to decode it — they skip it. <strong>Simplicity communicates faster, and speed is everything at the scroll.</strong></p><p>The thumbnails consistently pulling high CTR across competitive niches share one trait: a single, dominant visual idea. One face. One object. One question. One dramatic moment. Not five things competing for your eye — <em>one thing demanding your attention.</em></p><div class="highlight">"If you can remove it and the thumbnail still makes sense, remove it."</div><p>Applying "the less the better" doesn't mean lazy or empty thumbnails. It means <strong>ruthless editing</strong>. Start with everything you want to include, then cut until only the essential remains.</p>`
  },
  {
    tag:"Principle",title:"Minimal but Important: Minimalism in Thumbnails Is Discipline, Not Emptiness",meta:"By Victor Vandi · 5 min read · Principles",
    body:`<p>Minimalism is frequently misunderstood as simply "less stuff." That's reductive. <strong>True minimalism means every element that remains carries maximum weight.</strong> Nothing is decorative. Nothing is filler. What stays, stays because removing it would weaken the message.</p><p>In thumbnail design, "minimal but important" means you've made hard decisions. You chose one font instead of three — but it's the right font for the mood. You used two colours instead of seven — but they're the colours that create the highest contrast and emotional impact.</p><p>This principle also applies to <strong>text</strong>. If you include three words on a thumbnail, those three words must be so precisely chosen that they couldn't be improved by adding more. "I Tried It" hits differently than "I Tried This Method and Here's What Happened." Same information, completely different impact at a glance.</p><div class="highlight">"Minimalism requires more decisions, not fewer. You're choosing what to leave out — and that's harder than adding more."</div><p>The result of true minimalism is a thumbnail that feels <strong>confident</strong>. Cluttered thumbnails feel anxious. Minimal thumbnails feel authoritative — and that clarity builds trust before the click.</p>`
  },
  {
    tag:"Strategy",title:"Viewer Recognition: Train Your Audience to Identify Your Videos at a Glance",meta:"By Victor Vandi · 6 min read · Strategy",
    body:`<p>The goal of early-stage thumbnail design is to <strong>get the click</strong>. The goal of mature thumbnail design is something more powerful: making your thumbnails recognisable enough that your regular audience can spot your video in a crowded feed <em>before they read the title.</em></p><p>Recognition is built through consistency — not creative stagnation, but a <strong>visual system</strong> that persists across videos. Consistent colour palette, consistent font style, consistent layout structure, consistent use of your face or brand icon. Over time, this trains the brain to associate that visual signature with your content specifically.</p><p>Think of how MrBeast thumbnails are immediately identifiable. Or how a MKBHD thumbnail has a distinct look. These creators didn't arrive at that by accident — they built and maintained a visual language over hundreds of videos.</p><div class="highlight">"When your regular viewers can spot your video from the corner of their eye, you've built brand equity in the most valuable currency on YouTube."</div><p>For a growing channel: <strong>pick a visual system and stick to it for at least 20–30 videos.</strong> Evaluate CTR data. If it's working, refine — don't reinvent. Most channels that struggle have a different-looking thumbnail every upload, giving their audience nothing to recognise or anticipate.</p>`
  },
  {
    tag:"Format",title:"Before and After: The Thumbnail Format That Exploits the Brain's Love of Transformation",meta:"By Victor Vandi · 5 min read · Formats",
    body:`<p>The before-and-after thumbnail format is one of the most psychologically effective structures in content creation. It works because the human brain is neurologically wired to pay attention to <strong>change and transformation</strong>. Before/after thumbnails communicate a transformation instantly — without a word of explanation, the viewer's brain processes: "something changed, I want to know how."</p><p>The format works across almost any niche: fitness (body transformation), design (project before and after), tech (product comparison), cooking (ingredients vs. final dish), tutorials (messy code vs. clean code). Anywhere there's a start state and an end state, the format applies.</p><p>What makes it convert is <strong>specificity and credibility</strong>. The two states must be visually distinct enough that the difference is immediately obvious at thumbnail size. Subtle before/afters get lost. Dramatic ones stop the scroll.</p><div class="highlight">"Before and after thumbnails don't just show what the video is about — they show the size of the transformation, and viewers click for the journey."</div><p>Common mistake: making the "before" and "after" states equal in size. Give more visual weight to the <strong>after</strong> — it's the reward state, the aspirational end result.</p>`
  },
  {
    tag:"Visuals",title:"Action Shot: How Dynamic Frames Create Tension That Makes Viewers Click",meta:"By Victor Vandi · 5 min read · Formats",
    body:`<p>A static portrait of someone looking at the camera is safe. An <strong>action shot</strong> — someone mid-jump, mid-reaction, hands moving, eyes wide, caught in a moment — is dangerous in the best way. It implies movement. It implies consequence. It makes the viewer feel like they've stumbled upon a moment that's actively unfolding, and that they'll miss it if they don't click.</p><p>Action shots work because they create an <strong>open loop</strong> in the viewer's mind. A still frame of someone running makes you ask: where are they going? A hand reaching for something makes you ask: what are they grabbing? A face in shock makes you ask: what just happened?</p><p>For facecam creators, the most effective action shots are <strong>peak emotional expressions</strong>: genuine shock, laughter mid-word, visible frustration, jaw-drop surprise. These are hard to fake convincingly — which is why your best thumbnail moments are often pulled directly from genuine reactions caught on camera.</p><div class="highlight">"A still image tells the viewer what exists. An action shot makes them feel what's happening — and wanting to see what comes next."</div>`
  },
  {
    tag:"Ethics + Strategy",title:"No Clickbait: Why Deceptive Thumbnails Are a Short-Term Win That Destroys Long-Term Growth",meta:"By Victor Vandi · 6 min read · Principles",
    body:`<p>Clickbait gets clicks. That's the whole point — it's engineered to trigger a click reflex through exaggeration, false promise, or manufactured outrage. The problem is what happens immediately after the click: <strong>the viewer realises the thumbnail lied, and they leave.</strong></p><p>When viewers leave quickly, your Average View Duration drops. YouTube's algorithm interprets this as a signal that your content isn't satisfying the audience it was shown to — so it reduces distribution. The very click manipulation designed to boost your video ends up suppressing it.</p><p>The distinction between clickbait and compelling is simple: <strong>clickbait makes a promise the content doesn't keep. Compelling thumbnails make a promise the content exceeds.</strong> Both create the same tension and curiosity. Only one of them builds an audience.</p><div class="highlight">"The thumbnail earns the click. The content earns the return visit. Clickbait earns neither."</div><p>The long game on YouTube is <strong>repeat viewers and subscribers</strong>. Those people found you because you delivered on your promises. Clickbait never builds that audience — it just cycles through strangers who feel tricked.</p>`
  },
  {
    tag:"Optimisation",title:"Why A/B Testing: Stop Guessing Which Thumbnail Works — Let Data Tell You",meta:"By Victor Vandi · 6 min read · Optimisation",
    body:`<p>Thumbnail design is a creative process, but your opinions about which design is "better" are unreliable. You're not your audience. You have context they don't have, and aesthetic preferences shaped by your own exposure. <strong>A/B testing removes your bias from the equation and lets real audience behaviour answer the question.</strong></p><p>A/B testing means showing two different thumbnails for the same video to similar audiences and measuring which drives higher CTR. YouTube's built-in "Test & Compare" feature makes this straightforward — you upload two thumbnails, YouTube rotates them, and after sufficient impressions, you get performance data.</p><p>What should you test? Test <strong>one variable at a time</strong>: thumbnail with text vs. without, face visible vs. no face, warm colour palette vs. cool, action shot vs. static portrait. If you change multiple things simultaneously, you won't know which change caused the performance difference.</p><div class="highlight">"Designers have opinions. Data has answers."</div><p>Over time, A/B testing builds a picture of what your specific audience responds to. This is more valuable than any general advice about thumbnails — because your audience is specific.</p>`
  },
  {
    tag:"Technical",title:"Optimise for Mobile and TV: Your Thumbnail Must Work at Every Screen Size",meta:"By Victor Vandi · 5 min read · Technical",
    body:`<p>Here's a fact most thumbnail designers ignore: over 70% of YouTube views come from mobile devices, and TV (via YouTube on smart TVs) is the fastest-growing viewing surface. These are not just different screen sizes — they're completely different viewing contexts.</p><p>On mobile, your thumbnail appears at approximately <strong>168×94 pixels</strong> in the browse feed — roughly the size of a postage stamp on a phone screen. Text that reads clearly at full size becomes illegible. Faces that fill the frame on a laptop look microscopic on a phone. Details vanish. What survives is only the boldest, highest-contrast, most essential visual information.</p><p>On TV screens, the thumbnail appears larger — but the viewer is sitting 6–10 feet away. The effective resolution is similar to mobile in terms of perceived detail. <strong>High contrast, large subjects, and minimal text</strong> are the consistent requirements across both surfaces.</p><div class="highlight">"Design your thumbnail on a big screen. Judge it on a small one. If it doesn't work at mobile size, it doesn't work."</div><p>The practical test: before finalising any thumbnail, <strong>zoom out until it's roughly the size it appears in a phone feed</strong>. Can you still identify the subject clearly? Is the text (if any) readable? If not, simplify until it does.</p>`
  },
  {
    tag:"Brand",title:"Branding: How to Build a Thumbnail Identity That Makes Your Channel Unmistakable",meta:"By Victor Vandi · 6 min read · Brand & Aesthetics",
    body:`<p>Branding in thumbnails is not about slapping a logo in the corner. It's about building a <strong>visual signature</strong> — a set of consistent design choices that, over time, become associated with your channel in your viewer's memory. Done right, your brand makes your thumbnails recognisable even when your channel name is off-screen.</p><p>The core components of thumbnail branding are: <strong>colour palette</strong> (the 2–3 dominant colours you use consistently), <strong>typography</strong> (the specific font or font combination you use for text overlays), <strong>layout patterns</strong> (whether you tend to put your face on the left, text on the right, or vice versa), and <strong>style</strong> (cinematic, bold, minimal, playful — a tonal quality that persists across designs).</p><div class="highlight">"Brand isn't what you say you are. It's what someone instantly thinks when they see your thumbnail before they see your name."</div><p>The business case for branding: returning subscribers don't need to consciously search for your channel. A branded thumbnail in their feed triggers automatic recognition — and that recognition alone increases the likelihood of a click.</p>`
  },
  {
    tag:"Composition",title:"Rule of Thirds: The Composition Grid That Makes Thumbnails Naturally More Engaging",meta:"By Victor Vandi · 5 min read · Brand & Aesthetics",
    body:`<p>The rule of thirds is one of the foundational principles of visual composition — used in photography, film, painting, and graphic design for centuries. It's not a creative rule, it's a <strong>perceptual one</strong>: human vision finds images more balanced and interesting when key elements are placed at specific positions in the frame rather than dead centre.</p><p>The concept: divide your thumbnail into a 3×3 grid — three columns, three rows. You get four intersection points where the grid lines cross. <strong>These four points are where the human eye naturally travels first.</strong></p><p>Centred compositions feel static — technically correct but psychologically inert. Off-centre compositions (using the rule of thirds) create implied movement and space. If your subject faces right, place them on the left third — this gives visual breathing room in the direction they're facing and creates narrative momentum.</p><div class="highlight">"Dead-centre placement is the safe choice. The rule of thirds is the interesting one."</div><p>For thumbnails specifically: the rule of thirds also helps you designate space for text. Place your face or subject on one third, and text occupies the other — clean separation, no overlap, no competition.</p>`
  },
  {
    tag:"Design",title:"Colours: How to Use Colour Psychology and Contrast to Make Thumbnails That Stop the Scroll",meta:"By Victor Vandi · 6 min read · Brand & Aesthetics",
    body:`<p>Colour is the fastest communicator in any visual medium. Before the viewer processes your text or identifies your face, they've already absorbed the emotional temperature of your thumbnail's colour palette. <strong>Colour choices aren't aesthetic preferences — they're psychological decisions.</strong></p><p>Different colours carry consistent psychological associations: <strong>Red</strong> triggers urgency, danger, and excitement. <strong>Yellow</strong> signals energy, positivity, and attention-grabbing friendliness. <strong>Blue</strong> communicates trust, calm, and authority. <strong>Green</strong> signals growth, nature, and safety. <strong>Black</strong> carries sophistication, exclusivity, and cinematic weight.</p><p>In thumbnails, the most important colour principle is <strong>contrast</strong> — specifically, contrast between your subject and the background. High contrast creates visual separation that makes the subject pop even at small sizes.</p><div class="highlight">"Your colour palette is the first thing a viewer's brain processes, and the last thing most designers think about."</div><p>A practical system: choose a <strong>dominant colour</strong> (the background or largest area), an <strong>accent colour</strong> (used for text, borders, or highlights), and a <strong>neutral</strong> to balance. The accent should be high-contrast against the dominant.</p>`
  },
  {
    tag:"Strategy",title:"Don't Always Use Something New: Why Proven Formats Beat Creative Experimentation",meta:"By Victor Vandi · 5 min read · Advanced Tactics",
    body:`<p>Creative novelty feels exciting. But in thumbnail design, <strong>novelty without data is just risk.</strong> Formats become proven because they work — because they've been tested against millions of viewers across thousands of channels and consistently generated strong CTR. Abandoning a proven format for creative variety can quietly destroy your performance without you knowing why.</p><p>This is counterintuitive to creative people. We're conditioned to associate repetition with laziness. In reality, <strong>constraint is often the mother of quality</strong>. Having a consistent template forces you to execute better within a proven structure rather than spending creative energy reinventing the structure itself.</p><p>What "don't always use something new" means in practice: when you find a thumbnail format that produces strong CTR, <strong>extract the pattern and protect it</strong>. Was it the face-on-left, text-on-right layout? Was it the bold red background? Identify specifically what worked and replicate it intentionally.</p><div class="highlight">"Every time you scrap a proven format for creative novelty, you're paying for an experiment with your video's performance."</div>`
  },
  {
    tag:"Craft",title:"What Makes a Thumbnail Actually Clickable: Anatomy of a High-Converting Design",meta:"By Victor Vandi · 6 min read · Advanced Tactics",
    body:`<p>Not all eye-catching thumbnails are clickable, and not all clickable thumbnails are conventionally attractive. <strong>Clickable is a specific quality</strong> — it means the image creates enough curiosity, tension, or desire that a viewer in a scroll actively stops and chooses to click.</p><p>The four drivers of clickability are: <strong>Curiosity</strong> (the thumbnail raises a question the video answers), <strong>Desire</strong> (the thumbnail shows an outcome the viewer wants), <strong>Fear</strong> (the thumbnail implies a consequence the viewer wants to avoid), and <strong>Identity</strong> (the thumbnail signals that this video is for people like the viewer).</p><p>The strongest thumbnails often activate two or more of these simultaneously. A fitness transformation thumbnail activates Desire (the after body) and Curiosity (how did they do it). A "I made a huge mistake" thumbnail activates Curiosity and Fear simultaneously.</p><div class="highlight">"A clickable thumbnail doesn't describe the video. It makes the viewer feel something — and that feeling drives the click."</div><p>Technically: clickable thumbnails tend to share high contrast, a clear focal point, a face with strong expression, minimal text that adds context, and a colour palette that stands out in their feed context.</p>`
  },
  {
    tag:"Research",title:"Copy Cat: How to Study Competitors Ethically and Model What Actually Works",meta:"By Victor Vandi · 6 min read · Advanced Tactics",
    body:`<p>There's a difference between copying and modelling. <strong>Copying</strong> means reproducing someone else's thumbnail — same layout, same colours, same style. This is lazy and ultimately pointless because you're inheriting someone else's brand instead of building yours. <strong>Modelling</strong> means studying what structural and psychological decisions make successful thumbnails work, and applying those principles to your own original designs.</p><p>The copy cat approach, done right, is one of the most efficient ways to improve. Top-performing channels have done the testing for you. Their most-viewed videos have thumbnails that have proven themselves against millions of impressions.</p><p>What to study: <strong>layout patterns</strong>, <strong>colour choices</strong>, <strong>expression</strong>, and <strong>text strategy</strong>. Then ask: <em>why</em> does this work? What psychological trigger is it activating?</p><div class="highlight">"Model the principles, not the pixels. Understand why it works, then apply that understanding to your own original vision."</div><p>Practically: build a swipe file. Screenshot the thumbnails of top performers in your niche weekly. Annotate what design decisions you notice. Over time, patterns emerge — and those patterns are your niche's proven visual language.</p>`
  },
  {
    tag:"Title + Thumbnail",title:"Title — Perceived Value Loop: How Your Title and Thumbnail Work Together to Manufacture Irresistibility",meta:"By Victor Vandi · 7 min read · Advanced Tactics",
    body:`<p>A common mistake is designing thumbnails in isolation — as if the thumbnail alone determines whether someone clicks. In reality, the viewer sees <strong>two things simultaneously</strong>: the thumbnail and the title. These two elements function as a system, not as independent variables. When they work together, they create something greater than either alone: <strong>perceived value.</strong></p><p>Perceived value is the viewer's instant assessment of: "Is watching this video worth my time?" High perceived value = high CTR. Low perceived value = scroll past.</p><p>The <strong>loop</strong> concept means each element should reference and amplify the other. The thumbnail shows something intriguing that the title names. The title promises something specific that the thumbnail visualises. Neither one fully completes the story — the viewer has to click to close the loop.</p><div class="highlight">"The thumbnail is the question. The title is the hint. The video is the answer. Your job is to make the question irresistible."</div><p>Example: a thumbnail shows a shocked face next to a wrecked car. Title: "This Was My Biggest Mistake." The image raises a question. The title names the category but withholds the specifics. Together, they create a complete but unresolved story — and the only way to resolve it is to click.</p>`
  },
  {
    tag:"A/B Testing",title:"What A/B Testing Actually Means for Your Thumbnail",meta:"By Victor Vandi · 6 min read · A/B Testing",
    body:`<p>Most creators design a thumbnail, upload it, and then spend the next week either celebrating the views or wondering what went wrong. The variable they never think to examine is the thumbnail itself. That's what A/B testing fixes.</p><p><strong>A/B testing</strong> — also called split testing — is the practice of showing two or more different versions of a thumbnail to separate segments of your real audience and measuring which one drives better performance. Instead of guessing which design is stronger, you let actual viewer behaviour answer the question.</p><p>YouTube measures the winner based on <strong>watch time share</strong>, not just raw clicks. This matters — a clickbait thumbnail might win a pure CTR test but lose on watch time because the viewers it attracts leave quickly. Watch time share captures both the click and whether the viewer stuck around, making it a more honest measure of thumbnail quality.</p><div class="highlight">"A/B testing doesn't tell you what looks better. It tells you what performs better — with your specific audience, in your specific niche, right now."</div><p>Why does this matter so much? Because the same thumbnail that works brilliantly in a finance niche can completely fail in gaming. What resonates with your audience at 2am might not work at 2pm. A/B testing removes the assumption that general design principles apply universally to your channel — and gives you data that is specific, personal, and actionable.</p><p>The other underappreciated benefit: <strong>A/B testing compounds over time.</strong> Every test you run teaches you something about your audience's visual preferences. After 10 tests, you start to see patterns — maybe your audience always responds to close-up faces over wide shots, or warm backgrounds over cool ones. That accumulated knowledge is a genuine competitive advantage that no design tutorial can give you.</p>`
  },
  {
    tag:"A/B Testing",title:"How to Run Your First A/B Test Inside YouTube Studio",meta:"By Victor Vandi · 7 min read · A/B Testing",
    body:`<p>YouTube's built-in Test & Compare feature is one of the most underused tools on the platform. It's free, it's inside YouTube Studio, and it gives you real performance data on your thumbnails. Here's exactly how to use it.</p><h3>Step 1: Check Eligibility</h3><p>Test & Compare is available to channels that have <strong>advanced features enabled</strong> on YouTube. You also cannot test Shorts, scheduled lives, or videos marked as Made for Kids. If you don't see the feature yet, enable advanced features in your YouTube account settings and check back — YouTube has been rolling it out to more creators through 2024 and 2025.</p><h3>Step 2: Access the Test</h3><p>Go to <strong>YouTube Studio → Content → select your video → Details</strong>. In the Thumbnail section, click the three-dot menu and select "A/B testing." For new uploads, you'll see the testing option directly in the thumbnail section during setup.</p><h3>Step 3: Upload Your Variants</h3><p>You can test up to <strong>three thumbnails simultaneously</strong>. Upload your variants — YouTube will automatically rotate them to different random segments of your audience. Make sure each thumbnail is 1280×720 pixels, under 2MB, and clearly different enough from the others to generate a meaningful result. Thumbnails that are too similar can cause tests to run longer without a clear winner.</p><div class="highlight">"The test typically runs between a few hours and two weeks, depending on how many impressions your video receives."</div><h3>Step 4: Read the Results</h3><p>Check your results in <strong>YouTube Studio → Analytics → Reach tab</strong>. You'll see one of three outcomes: Winner (one thumbnail clearly outperformed), Performed the Same, or Inconclusive. YouTube will automatically apply the winning thumbnail — or default to your first upload if there's no clear winner.</p><h3>Step 5: Record and Learn</h3><p>Don't just accept the result and move on. Note exactly what was different about the winning thumbnail. Over multiple tests, these notes become a playbook for your channel's visual language.</p>`
  },
  {
    tag:"A/B Testing",title:"The One Variable Rule: Why You Must Only Change One Thing Per Test",meta:"By Victor Vandi · 5 min read · A/B Testing",
    body:`<p>Here's the most common A/B testing mistake: a creator designs two thumbnails, and between them they've changed the background colour, swapped the face for an object, removed the text, and shifted the entire layout. The test runs. One wins. And the creator learns absolutely nothing useful.</p><p>If you change multiple things between two thumbnails, you cannot know <strong>which change caused the performance difference.</strong> Was it the colour? The face? The text? The layout? You have no idea — which means you can't apply the learning to your next video. You've spent two weeks running a test that produced noise instead of signal.</p><p>The One Variable Rule is simple: <strong>between any two test thumbnails, change exactly one thing.</strong> Everything else stays identical. Test face vs. no face — keep the background, text, and layout the same. Test warm vs. cool background — keep the face, expression, and text the same. Test three words vs. no text — keep everything else constant.</p><div class="highlight">"A test that changes everything teaches you nothing. A test that changes one thing teaches you something you can use forever."</div><p>This requires discipline, especially when you have a strong intuition that a completely different design would perform better. Run that instinct as a separate test later. For now, isolate the variable. The slower, more methodical approach builds a library of reliable knowledge — and that library grows into a genuine competitive advantage over creators who just guess.</p><p>Practical tip: build a <strong>base template</strong> for your thumbnails — fixed layout, fixed font, fixed colour palette. Then each A/B test becomes a clean swap of one element within that template. This makes the One Variable Rule easy to apply, and also naturally improves your brand consistency at the same time.</p>`
  },
  {
    tag:"A/B Testing",title:"Reading Your Results: What Winner, Inconclusive and Performed the Same Actually Mean",meta:"By Victor Vandi · 6 min read · A/B Testing",
    body:`<p>After your YouTube A/B test runs, you'll get one of three results. Most creators either misread these outcomes or don't know how to act on them. Here's what each one actually means.</p><h3>Winner</h3><p>This is the clearest result. YouTube is telling you that <strong>one thumbnail clearly outperformed the others based on watch time share</strong>, and the difference is statistically significant — meaning it's very unlikely to be random. YouTube will automatically apply this thumbnail to your video. Your job: note exactly what made it different from the losing version. That observation goes into your playbook.</p><h3>Performed the Same</h3><p>This means the test ran, both thumbnails received impressions, and <strong>there was no meaningful difference between them.</strong> This is not a failed test — it's data. It tells you that whatever you changed doesn't matter to your specific audience. That's useful. Stop spending time on that variable and test something more impactful instead.</p><div class="highlight">"Inconclusive isn't failure. Winner isn't always right. Learning to read each outcome correctly is half the value of testing."</div><h3>Inconclusive</h3><p>Inconclusive means <strong>there wasn't enough data to reach statistical confidence</strong>. This usually happens when the video didn't receive enough impressions during the test window, or when the thumbnails were too similar for a difference to emerge clearly. When this happens: either run the test again on a higher-traffic video, make the thumbnail variants more visually distinct, or accept that this particular test won't yield a reliable answer and move on.</p><p>One important nuance: YouTube measures victory by <strong>watch time share, not pure CTR</strong>. A thumbnail can attract more clicks but still lose the test if those viewers leave quickly. This means the "winner" is the thumbnail that brings in viewers who actually watch — which is the right metric to optimise for.</p>`
  },
  {
    tag:"A/B Testing",title:"How to Use A/B Testing to Revive Old Videos That Stopped Performing",meta:"By Victor Vandi · 7 min read · A/B Testing",
    body:`<p>Your back catalogue is one of the most underused assets on your channel. Older videos that have gone quiet — sitting at 15,000 views with no new traffic — are not necessarily dead. They may just have the wrong thumbnail. A strategic A/B test can restart an old video's algorithm momentum entirely.</p><p>Here's why this works: YouTube's algorithm never fully stops evaluating your content. It continues showing your videos to new audiences periodically, testing whether they perform. If the CTR is too low, the video gets shown less. <strong>Improving the thumbnail raises the CTR, which signals to the algorithm that the video is worth distributing again</strong> — and distribution picks back up.</p><div class="highlight">"I've seen videos stuck at 15K views for months hit 120K after a single thumbnail swap. The content didn't change. The packaging did."</div><p>The approach for testing old videos is slightly different from new ones. For new uploads, you're optimising within a launch window where early performance matters most. For old videos, you're playing a longer game — testing over weeks rather than days, and looking for a sustained lift in impressions rather than a spike.</p><h3>Which Old Videos to Test First</h3><p>Prioritise videos that have <strong>high search volume topics but low CTR</strong> — you can check this in YouTube Studio Analytics under the Reach tab. These are videos where the content is what people are searching for, but the thumbnail isn't convincing them to click. That gap is exactly what a better thumbnail can fix.</p><h3>What to Test on Old Videos</h3><p>Start with the biggest possible visual change: if the original uses a wide shot, test a close-up face. If the original has no text, test adding three words. If the original is warm-toned, test a cool palette. You're looking for a meaningful shift in performance, not a marginal improvement — so make the variants genuinely different.</p><p>YouTube recommends testing older videos first precisely because the impact to your channel's live performance is lower. Use that lower-risk environment to learn, then apply those lessons to your new uploads where the stakes are higher.</p>`
  },
  {
    tag:"Web Strategy",title:"Your Website Is Your Best Salesperson — And It Never Sleeps",meta:"By Victor Vandi · 5 min read · Web Strategy",
    body:`<p>Think about what a great salesperson does: they answer questions, overcome objections, communicate value, and guide a prospect toward a decision — all without you being in the room. A great website does exactly the same thing, at any hour, for any number of visitors simultaneously.</p><p>The difference is that a salesperson gets tired, takes days off, and has bad days. A well-built website doesn't. It delivers the same pitch, the same quality of information, and the same conversion experience at 3am on a Sunday as it does at 10am on a Monday.</p><div class="highlight">"Most businesses spend enormous energy hiring and training people to sell — then build a website that actively undermines them. Your website is either selling for you or working against you. There's no neutral."</div><h3>What a Sales-Focused Website Actually Does</h3><p><strong>It answers questions before they're asked.</strong> Every visitor arrives with objections — Is this trustworthy? Is it worth the price? Will it work for my situation? A website that anticipates and answers these questions removes the friction between interest and action.</p><p><strong>It communicates value immediately.</strong> Within five seconds of landing on your site, a visitor should understand exactly what you do, who it's for, and why they should care. If that clarity isn't there, they leave — and they don't come back.</p><p><strong>It builds trust through evidence.</strong> Testimonials, case studies, real results, and social proof aren't decorative — they're the equivalent of a reference check. They answer the question every visitor is silently asking: "Has this worked for someone like me?"</p><h3>The Cost of Getting It Wrong</h3><p>A website that doesn't convert isn't just passive — it's actively losing you business. Every visitor who arrives, gets confused, and leaves is a lead your competitors are now free to close. The cost of a non-performing website isn't just the money spent building it — it's every sale it failed to generate.</p>`
  },
  {
    tag:"UX",title:"The First 5 Seconds: How Visitors Judge Your Entire Business in One Glance",meta:"By Victor Vandi · 4 min read · UX",
    body:`<p>Research by Google found that users form an aesthetic judgment about a website in as little as 50 milliseconds — faster than a conscious thought can form. By the time someone has read your headline, a decision about whether to trust you is already half-made.</p><p>This isn't a design opinion. It's cognitive science. The human brain is a pattern-recognition machine, and it's constantly running background assessments: "Does this look legitimate? Is this the kind of place I want to do business with? Does this feel competent or amateur?"</p><div class="highlight">"Your website's first impression isn't formed by your content — it's formed by your visual credibility. Content gets read by people who already decided to stay."</div><h3>What Visitors Are Actually Reacting To</h3><p><strong>Visual hierarchy:</strong> Is there a clear flow from the most important element to the next? Or does the eye land randomly on a cluttered page with no clear center of gravity?</p><p><strong>Whitespace and breathing room:</strong> Crowded layouts feel anxious and untrustworthy. Spacious layouts feel confident and established. This isn't aesthetic preference — it's a legibility and trust signal.</p><p><strong>Typography confidence:</strong> Small, thin, hard-to-read fonts communicate uncertainty. Large, clear, well-spaced typography communicates authority.</p><p><strong>Colour consistency:</strong> A page that uses seven different colours with no system looks accidental. A page with two or three colours used deliberately looks intentional — and intentional reads as professional.</p><h3>The Practical Fix</h3><p>Before worrying about content, copy, or SEO, ask this: if a stranger landed on your homepage and had 5 seconds, would they know exactly what you do and want to stay? Print your homepage at phone size and look at it cold. The answer will be obvious.</p>`
  },
  {
    tag:"Performance",title:"Page Speed Is Not a Tech Problem — It's a Revenue Problem",meta:"By Victor Vandi · 5 min read · Performance",
    body:`<p>Amazon found that every 100 milliseconds of additional load time cost them 1% in revenue. Google's own data shows that as page load time increases from 1 second to 3 seconds, the probability of a visitor bouncing increases by 32%. At 5 seconds, that bounce probability jumps by 90%.</p><p>These aren't edge cases or hypotheticals. They're documented patterns across millions of user sessions. Yet most business owners treat website speed as a technical concern — something for developers to manage — rather than a direct driver of revenue.</p><div class="highlight">"Your website's speed is a product decision, not a technical one. Every second of delay is a deliberate choice to make your visitors' experience worse — and your conversion rate lower."</div><h3>Why Speed Feels Like a Technical Problem But Isn't</h3><p>Speed is caused by technical factors: image sizes, server response times, unoptimised code, too many third-party scripts. But the business decision is upstream: how much do you invest in solving it? A fast website requires intentional choices — using a quality host, compressing images properly, writing lean code, avoiding bloated page builders.</p><p>Those are business decisions disguised as technical ones. The cost of not making them is paid in conversion rates, not in error logs.</p><h3>What Good Speed Looks Like</h3><p>A well-built website should load in under 2 seconds on a standard mobile connection. Google's Core Web Vitals — Largest Contentful Paint, First Input Delay, and Cumulative Layout Shift — are measurable targets. A site that passes these tests is competitive. One that fails is quietly losing to every competitor whose site loads faster.</p><p>The test is free: run your site through Google PageSpeed Insights right now. The number you see is a direct proxy for how much friction you're creating before a visitor even reads your first word.</p>`
  },
  {
    tag:"SEO",title:"SEO Is Not Magic: What Actually Moves You Up in Google",meta:"By Victor Vandi · 6 min read · SEO",
    body:`<p>Search Engine Optimisation has a reputation for being mysterious, manipulative, and constantly changing. That reputation exists because a lot of people selling SEO services benefit from the mystery. The actual principles are simpler, more stable, and more achievable than the industry often admits.</p><p>Google's core goal hasn't changed in twenty years: return the most relevant, trustworthy, useful result for any given search. Everything Google's algorithm does is in service of that goal. Understanding that goal is most of what you need to know about SEO.</p><div class="highlight">"If your page genuinely answers the question better than anyone else's page, Google's incentive is to show it first. SEO is mostly about making sure Google can find, understand, and trust what you've already built."</div><h3>The Three Things That Actually Matter</h3><p><strong>Relevance:</strong> Does your page clearly address the topic someone is searching for? This means using the language your audience actually uses, covering the topic with genuine depth, and structuring content so Google can understand what it's about.</p><p><strong>Authority:</strong> Does Google have reason to trust your site? Authority is built through links from other credible sites, consistent publication of useful content, and a domain history that signals legitimacy. It's slow to build and hard to fake.</p><p><strong>Technical accessibility:</strong> Can Google actually crawl and index your pages? Fast load times, mobile-friendly design, clean code, and proper sitemap structure all fall under this category. Most well-built modern websites handle this automatically.</p><h3>What Doesn't Work</h3><p>Keyword stuffing, buying backlinks, hiding text, and other manipulation tactics either don't work or actively result in penalties. Google's ability to detect these approaches has improved every year. The ROI on legitimate SEO — genuinely useful content, well-built pages, real audience value — consistently outperforms shortcut tactics over any meaningful time horizon.</p>`
  },
  {
    tag:"Conversion",title:"The Anatomy of a High-Converting Landing Page",meta:"By Victor Vandi · 6 min read · Conversion",
    body:`<p>A landing page has one job: convert a visitor into a lead or a customer. Not inform. Not impress. Convert. Every design decision, every word of copy, every button placement should be evaluated against that single criterion.</p><p>Most landing pages fail because they try to do too many things. They include navigation that leads visitors away, sections that aren't relevant to the conversion goal, and CTAs buried so far down the page that only the most determined visitors find them.</p><div class="highlight">"A landing page is the most important single page you'll ever build. It should be so focused that removing any element would reduce conversion — and so clear that a distracted person on a phone can understand it in under 10 seconds."</div><h3>The Elements That Drive Conversion</h3><p><strong>The headline:</strong> One sentence. It must answer "what is this and why should I care?" immediately. If your headline requires context to understand, rewrite it.</p><p><strong>The subheadline:</strong> Expands the headline with the specific promise or benefit. Where the headline creates interest, the subheadline earns the next scroll.</p><p><strong>The primary CTA:</strong> Above the fold, always. One action, clearly labelled. "Get Started" is weak. "Book Your Free Call" is specific and carries a value promise.</p><p><strong>Social proof:</strong> Testimonials, client logos, review counts, case study results. Positioned near the CTA, not buried at the bottom. Social proof reduces the risk a visitor perceives in taking action.</p><p><strong>Objection handling:</strong> Every visitor has objections — price, time commitment, whether it'll work for them. The landing page's job is to identify the most common objections and answer them directly, before the visitor has a chance to leave.</p><h3>What to Remove</h3><p>Navigation menus. Sidebar content. Social media links. Anything that can pull a visitor away from the conversion action. A landing page with outbound navigation is a landing page leaking leads.</p>`
  },
  {
    tag:"Web Strategy",title:"A Beautiful Website That Doesn't Work Is Just Expensive Art",meta:"By Victor Vandi · 5 min read · Web Strategy",
    body:`<p>There's a category of website that wins design awards, looks stunning in a portfolio, and generates almost no business. It has breathtaking visuals, intricate animations, and careful typography. It also has a contact form that doesn't submit, a mobile layout that breaks, and a load time that drives visitors away before the first image finishes rendering.</p><p>This is expensive art. It may accurately represent the aesthetic vision of the people who built it. It does not function as a business tool.</p><div class="highlight">"Design without function is decoration. In business, decoration doesn't pay the bills. Conversion does."</div><h3>Where Beautiful-But-Broken Websites Come From</h3><p>They usually come from a design process that prioritises how a site looks in a static mockup over how it behaves in a real browser on a real device with a real user trying to accomplish something specific. Designers who have never done user testing often build for screenshots, not for use.</p><p>The result is a site that looks perfect in a Figma file and performs poorly in practice — because Figma files don't have to load in 2 seconds, don't have to work on a 4G connection in Lagos, and don't have to guide a confused first-time visitor to a CTA they can find.</p><h3>The Standard That Actually Matters</h3><p>A website works when real users — not the people who built it — can accomplish their goals without friction. They can find what they need, understand what the business offers, and take action without confusion or technical failure. That standard is measurable through user testing, analytics, and conversion data. It's harder to achieve than a beautiful static design. It's also the only standard that generates a return on investment.</p>`
  },
  {
    tag:"Web Strategy",title:"Why Functionality Beats Pure Design for Real Business Growth",meta:"By Victor Vandi · 5 min read · Web Strategy",
    body:`<p>A website that generates consistent leads with average visuals will always outperform a stunning website that generates none. This is obvious when stated directly, and yet the majority of website briefs prioritise "looking professional" or "standing out visually" over the functional requirements that actually drive business outcomes.</p><p>This isn't an argument against good design. Design matters enormously — it builds trust, communicates professionalism, and reduces friction. The argument is about priority: function must be the foundation on which design is built, not an afterthought once the visuals are approved.</p><div class="highlight">"A website that is fully functional and average-looking will outperform a broken website with stunning design every single time. Start with function. Layer design on top. Never reverse that order."</div><h3>What Function Actually Means</h3><p>A functional website does specific things reliably: it loads quickly on all devices, forms submit correctly, CTAs lead where they should, content is findable, and the path from landing to conversion is clear and unobstructed. None of these are design concerns — they're engineering and product concerns. But they are the ceiling on how much a beautiful design can achieve.</p><h3>The Test of a Functional Website</h3><p>Give your website to five people who have never seen it and ask them to complete a specific task — find the pricing, contact you, understand your services. Watch where they get confused, where they get stuck, where they give up. The problems they surface are functional problems. Fix those before you spend another hour on visual refinement. Design optimisation on a functionally broken website is waste.</p>`
  },
  {
    tag:"Web Strategy",title:"The Real Cost of a Slow, Broken, or Confusing Website",meta:"By Victor Vandi · 4 min read · Web Strategy",
    body:`<p>When a business owner looks at their website costs, they typically see: hosting fee, design fee, development fee, maintenance fee. What they almost never see is the cost of every visitor who arrived, couldn't find what they needed, and left — permanently.</p><p>That invisible cost is usually larger than all the visible ones combined. And unlike the visible costs, it compounds: a visitor who had a bad experience doesn't just leave — they form a lasting negative impression of the business, they don't recommend it, and they don't come back.</p><div class="highlight">"The cost of a bad website isn't in your invoice. It's in your analytics. Every bounce, every abandoned form, every visitor who left without converting is a number your P&L will never show you — but your competitors are collecting."</div><h3>The Three Ways a Website Loses You Money</h3><p><strong>Slow load times:</strong> For every second of load time above 2 seconds, conversion rates drop measurably. A 5-second load time can cost you more than 40% of visitors before they've seen a single word of your content.</p><p><strong>Broken functionality:</strong> Forms that don't submit. Buttons that don't click. Pages that 404. Each broken element doesn't just create friction — it signals incompetence. A broken website tells a visitor that attention to detail isn't a priority here, which immediately raises questions about the quality of the actual product or service.</p><p><strong>Confusing navigation:</strong> If a visitor can't find what they're looking for in under 3 clicks, they leave. Not because they're impatient, but because their time has value and your site is signalling that it doesn't respect it.</p><h3>Quantifying the Problem</h3><p>Run a simple calculation: how many visitors does your site receive per month? What percentage convert? What is each conversion worth? Now reduce your conversion rate by 30% — that's a conservative estimate of what a slow, broken, or confusing website costs. For most businesses, that number is significant enough to justify fixing the problem this week.</p>`
  },
  {
    tag:"Web Strategy",title:"Mobile-First Is Not a Trend — It's a Business Requirement",meta:"By Victor Vandi · 4 min read · Web Strategy",
    body:`<p>As of 2024, over 60% of all global web traffic comes from mobile devices. In Nigeria and across sub-Saharan Africa, that number is closer to 80%. The majority of the people who will ever visit your website will do so on a phone, often on a mobile data connection, often while doing something else at the same time.</p><p>A website that works perfectly on a desktop but breaks, compresses, overflows, or slows to a crawl on mobile is a website that is failing the majority of its visitors every day. That is not a small technical problem — it is a fundamental business failure.</p><div class="highlight">"Mobile-first isn't a design philosophy. It's an acknowledgment of reality: most of your visitors are on a phone. Build for them first, and treat desktop as the upgrade — not the other way around."</div><h3>What Mobile-First Actually Means</h3><p>It means designing and testing the mobile experience before the desktop experience. It means images that load efficiently on a 4G connection. It means tap targets large enough to hit with a thumb, text large enough to read without zooming, and layouts that don't require horizontal scrolling.</p><p>It also means accepting that some design elements that look great on a wide desktop screen simply don't translate to mobile — and making the disciplined choice to remove or redesign them rather than force them to fit.</p><h3>How to Test It</h3><p>Open your website on your own phone. Navigate it as a first-time visitor would — find your contact information, understand your pricing, try to submit a form. Time how long it takes to load. Note every moment of friction. That experience is what the majority of your visitors have every day. If it frustrates you, it's frustrating them — and they have competitors' sites one back-button away.</p>`
  },
  {
    tag:"Web Strategy",title:"Conversion Rate: The One Number That Proves Your Website Actually Works",meta:"By Victor Vandi · 5 min read · Web Strategy",
    body:`<p>A website can have beautiful design, strong SEO, consistent traffic, and still be failing. The number that reveals the truth is conversion rate: the percentage of visitors who take the action you want them to take. Everything else is context. Conversion rate is the verdict.</p><p>Most business owners don't know their website's conversion rate. They know their traffic numbers — monthly visitors, page views, sessions — because those numbers are visible and they tend to go up. Conversion rate requires more attention to find, and it often tells a less comfortable story.</p><div class="highlight">"Traffic is flattering. Conversion rate is honest. A thousand visitors a month who never convert are worth less than a hundred who do. Build for conversion, not for traffic."</div><h3>What Good Conversion Rates Look Like</h3><p>Average website conversion rates vary significantly by industry and goal type. For lead generation forms on service businesses, 2–5% is typical; well-optimised pages can reach 10–15%. E-commerce sites average 1–3%. If you don't know your current rate, calculating it is the first step: divide the number of conversions (form submissions, calls, purchases) by your total visitor count and multiply by 100.</p><h3>What Affects Conversion Rate</h3><p>Almost everything: load speed, clarity of the value proposition, quality of social proof, position and wording of CTAs, form length, mobile experience, and page layout all affect whether a visitor converts. This is why conversion rate optimisation is a discipline rather than a single fix — it's the ongoing process of identifying and removing the specific friction points that are costing you conversions.</p><h3>Why It's the Most Important Metric</h3><p>Because improving conversion rate is the highest-leverage action available to most businesses with an existing website. Doubling your traffic requires significant investment in marketing. Doubling your conversion rate requires understanding your visitors better — and that understanding is available in your existing analytics, right now, for free.</p>`
  }
];

function openArticle(i) {
  const a = articles[i];
  document.getElementById('modal-tag').textContent = a.tag;
  document.getElementById('modal-title').textContent = a.title;
  document.getElementById('modal-meta').textContent = a.meta;
  document.getElementById('modal-body').innerHTML = a.body;
  document.getElementById('modal').classList.add('open');
  document.body.style.overflow = 'hidden';
  document.getElementById('modal-box').scrollTop = 0;
}
function closeArticle() {
  document.getElementById('modal').classList.remove('open');
  document.body.style.overflow = '';
}
function closeOnOverlay(e) { if (e.target === document.getElementById('modal')) closeArticle(); }
document.addEventListener('keydown', e => { if (e.key === 'Escape') closeArticle(); });

// Nav always scrolled style in SPA
document.getElementById('navbar').classList.add('scrolled');

// Skill bar animation
const skillObserver = new IntersectionObserver(entries => {
  entries.forEach(entry => {
    if (entry.isIntersecting) {
      entry.target.querySelectorAll('.skill-bar-fill').forEach(bar => bar.classList.add('animated'));
      skillObserver.unobserve(entry.target);
    }
  });
}, { threshold: 0.2 });
document.querySelectorAll('#skills .section').forEach(el => skillObserver.observe(el));

// Scroll reveal
const reveals = document.querySelectorAll('.reveal');
const revealObserver = new IntersectionObserver(entries => {
  entries.forEach((entry, i) => {
    if (entry.isIntersecting) {
      setTimeout(() => entry.target.classList.add('visible'), i * 60);
      revealObserver.unobserve(entry.target);
    }
  });
}, { threshold: 0.08 });
reveals.forEach(el => revealObserver.observe(el));
/* ══ CREATIVE JS ══ */

// Cursor — enabled only on fine-pointer devices and guarded for reliability
const cursor = document.getElementById('cursor');
const ring   = document.getElementById('cursorRing');
const supportsFinePointer = window.matchMedia && window.matchMedia('(pointer: fine)').matches;
if (supportsFinePointer && cursor && ring) {
  document.body.classList.add('custom-cursor-enabled');
  let mx=0, my=0, rx=0, ry=0;
  document.addEventListener('mousemove', e => {
    mx=e.clientX; my=e.clientY;
    cursor.style.left=mx+'px'; cursor.style.top=my+'px';
  }, { passive:true });
  (function animRing(){
    rx += (mx-rx)*0.12; ry += (my-ry)*0.12;
    ring.style.left=rx+'px'; ring.style.top=ry+'px';
    requestAnimationFrame(animRing);
  })();
  document.querySelectorAll('a,button,.article-card,.pkg-card,.thumb-wrap').forEach(el=>{
    el.addEventListener('mouseenter',()=>ring.classList.add('expand'));
    el.addEventListener('mouseleave',()=>ring.classList.remove('expand'));
  });
} else {
  if (cursor) cursor.remove();
  if (ring) ring.remove();
}

// Scroll progress bar
const prog = document.getElementById('scroll-progress');
window.addEventListener('scroll',()=>{
  const s = document.documentElement;
  const p = s.scrollTop/(s.scrollHeight-s.clientHeight)*100;
  prog.style.width = p+'%';
});

// ─── VICTOR VANDI — COMPLETE ANIMATED HERO ─────────────────────────────────
(function initHero3D(){
  if(typeof THREE === 'undefined') return;

  const isMobile = /Mobi|Android|iPhone|iPad/i.test(navigator.userAgent) || window.innerWidth < 768;
  const dpr      = Math.min(window.devicePixelRatio || 1, isMobile ? 1.5 : 2);
  const prefersRM= window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;

  const container = document.getElementById('hero-canvas');
  if(!container) return;
  const renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({ antialias: !isMobile, alpha: true });
  renderer.setPixelRatio(dpr);
  renderer.setClearColor(0x000000, 0);
  const W = container.offsetWidth || window.innerWidth;
  const H = container.offsetHeight || window.innerHeight;
  renderer.setSize(W, H);
  container.appendChild(renderer.domElement);

  const scene  = new THREE.Scene();
  const camera = new THREE.PerspectiveCamera(isMobile ? 65 : 55, W / H, 0.1, 350);
  camera.position.set(0, 0, 0);
  scene.fog = new THREE.FogExp2(0x07080a, isMobile ? 0.025 : 0.020);

  const C_ORANGE = new THREE.Color(0xd4622a);
  const C_AMBER  = new THREE.Color(0xf0a840);
  const C_WHITE  = new THREE.Color(0xe8eaf0);

  // ── THUMBNAIL FRAME CORRIDOR ─────────────────────────────────────────────
  const ALL_FRAMES = [
    [-3.0, 0.5,-16, 0.32, 0.04,1.00,'solid'],
    [ 3.2,-0.5,-24,-0.28, 0.00,0.90,'wire' ],
    [-2.6, 1.4,-36, 0.24, 0.08,1.05,'solid'],
    [ 3.5, 0.2,-46,-0.20, 0.00,0.85,'wire' ],
    [-3.4,-0.6,-58, 0.36,-0.04,1.00,'solid'],
    [ 2.8, 1.0,-68,-0.30, 0.00,0.80,'wire' ],
    [-2.2, 0.0,-80, 0.18, 0.00,1.20,'solid'],
    [ 3.8,-0.8,-92,-0.25, 0.05,1.00,'wire' ],
    [-3.0, 0.9,-106,0.30, 0.00,0.95,'solid'],
    [ 2.6, 0.4,-118,-0.22,0.00,1.00,'wire' ],
    [ 0.0, 2.5,-130,0.00,-0.18,1.30,'solid'],
    [-3.6,-0.3,-144,0.35, 0.03,1.00,'wire' ],
    [ 3.0, 0.6,-156,-0.28,0.00,0.90,'solid'],
  ];
  const frameDefs    = isMobile ? ALL_FRAMES.slice(0,7) : ALL_FRAMES;
  const frameObjects = [];
  const frameGroup   = new THREE.Group();
  scene.add(frameGroup);

  frameDefs.forEach(function(fd){
    var x=fd[0],y=fd[1],z=fd[2],ry=fd[3],rx=fd[4],sc=fd[5],type=fd[6];
    var w=1.78*sc, h=sc;
    if(type === 'solid'){
      var geo = new THREE.PlaneGeometry(w,h);
      var mat = new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({color:C_ORANGE,transparent:true,opacity:0.07,side:THREE.DoubleSide});
      var pl  = new THREE.Mesh(geo,mat);
      pl.position.set(x,y,z); pl.rotation.set(rx,ry,0);
      frameGroup.add(pl);
      var em = new THREE.LineBasicMaterial({color:C_ORANGE,transparent:true,opacity:0.50});
      pl.add(new THREE.LineSegments(new THREE.EdgesGeometry(geo),em));
      [[ w/2, h/2],[-(w/2), h/2],[ w/2,-(h/2)],[-(w/2),-(h/2)]].forEach(function(c){
        var d=new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.SphereGeometry(0.015,4,4),new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({color:C_AMBER}));
        d.position.set(c[0],c[1],0.001); pl.add(d);
      });
      var tri=new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.ConeGeometry(0.06,0.10,3),
        new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({color:C_AMBER,transparent:true,opacity:0.25}));
      tri.rotation.z=-Math.PI/2; tri.position.set(0.02,0,0.001); pl.add(tri);
      frameObjects.push({mesh:pl,mat:em,z:z,base:0.50});
    } else {
      var geo2=new THREE.PlaneGeometry(w,h);
      var fm=new THREE.LineBasicMaterial({color:C_AMBER,transparent:true,opacity:0.25});
      var fr=new THREE.LineSegments(new THREE.EdgesGeometry(geo2),fm);
      fr.position.set(x,y,z); fr.rotation.set(rx,ry,0);
      frameGroup.add(fr);
      frameObjects.push({mesh:fr,mat:fm,z:z,base:0.25});
    }
  });

  // ── FLOATING ORBITAL ACCENTS ─────────────────────────────────────────────
  var orbitals = [];
  if(!isMobile){
    var orbDefs = [
      {geo:new THREE.OctahedronGeometry(0.08), x:-5.5,y: 2.0,z:-8,  col:C_ORANGE,spd:0.6},
      {geo:new THREE.TetrahedronGeometry(0.07), x: 5.2,y: 1.5,z:-10, col:C_AMBER, spd:0.8},
      {geo:new THREE.IcosahedronGeometry(0.06), x:-4.8,y:-2.0,z:-12, col:C_ORANGE,spd:0.5},
      {geo:new THREE.OctahedronGeometry(0.05),  x: 4.5,y:-1.8,z:-7,  col:C_AMBER, spd:0.9},
    ];
    orbDefs.forEach(function(d){
      var m=new THREE.Mesh(d.geo,new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({color:d.col,wireframe:true,transparent:true,opacity:0.35}));
      m.position.set(d.x,d.y,d.z);
      m._spd=d.spd; m._origY=d.y;
      scene.add(m); orbitals.push(m);
    });
  }

  // ── PARTICLE FIELD ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  var N=isMobile?900:1800;
  var pGeo=new THREE.BufferGeometry();
  var pPos=new Float32Array(N*3), pCol=new Float32Array(N*3);
  for(var i=0;i<N;i++){
    var a=Math.random()*Math.PI*2, r=1.5+Math.random()*10;
    pPos[i*3]=Math.cos(a)*r; pPos[i*3+1]=Math.sin(a)*r; pPos[i*3+2]=(Math.random()-.2)*160;
    var t=Math.random(), c=t<.55?C_ORANGE:(t<.80?C_AMBER:C_WHITE);
    pCol[i*3]=c.r; pCol[i*3+1]=c.g; pCol[i*3+2]=c.b;
  }
  pGeo.setAttribute('position',new THREE.BufferAttribute(pPos,3));
  pGeo.setAttribute('color',   new THREE.BufferAttribute(pCol,3));
  scene.add(new THREE.Points(pGeo,new THREE.PointsMaterial({
    size:isMobile?.07:.055,vertexColors:true,transparent:true,opacity:.70,sizeAttenuation:true
  })));

  // ── DEPTH STREAKS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  var sN=isMobile?10:20;
  for(var si=0;si<sN;si++){
    var sa=(si/sN)*Math.PI*2, sr=5+Math.random()*5;
    var zS=-8-Math.random()*70, zE=zS-6-Math.random()*18;
    var pts=[new THREE.Vector3(Math.cos(sa)*sr,Math.sin(sa)*sr,zS),
             new THREE.Vector3(Math.cos(sa)*sr*.5,Math.sin(sa)*sr*.5,zE)];
    scene.add(new THREE.Line(new THREE.BufferGeometry().setFromPoints(pts),
      new THREE.LineBasicMaterial({color:si%2?C_AMBER:C_ORANGE,transparent:true,opacity:.13})));
  }

  // ── 3D CURSOR ORB ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  var orbMesh=new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.SphereGeometry(0.13,10,10),
    new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({color:C_ORANGE,transparent:true,opacity:0}));
  var haloMesh=new THREE.Mesh(new THREE.TorusGeometry(0.26,0.013,8,32),
    new THREE.MeshBasicMaterial({color:C_AMBER,transparent:true,opacity:0}));
  orbMesh.add(haloMesh); scene.add(orbMesh);
  orbMesh.position.set(0,0,-3);

  // ── HTML INJECTIONS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  var heroEl = document.querySelector('.hero');
  var glowEl = document.createElement('div');
  glowEl.id  = 'hero-cursor-glow'; heroEl.appendChild(glowEl);
  var frameLabel = document.createElement('div');
  frameLabel.id  = 'hero-frame-label'; frameLabel.textContent = '▶ THUMBNAIL';
  heroEl.appendChild(frameLabel);

  // ── STATE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  var mouseX=0,mouseY=0,rawMX=0,rawMY=0,smoothMX=0,smoothMY=0;
  var targetCamZ=0, camZ=prefersRM?0:9;
  var orbOpacity=0, idleTimer=0, lastMouseX=0;
  var TUNNEL=156;

  function onMouse(e){
    var rect=heroEl.getBoundingClientRect();
    var cx=(e.clientX||(e.touches&&e.touches[0]&&e.touches[0].clientX)||0);
    var cy=(e.clientY||(e.touches&&e.touches[0]&&e.touches[0].clientY)||0);
    mouseX=(cx/window.innerWidth-.5)*2;
    mouseY=(cy/window.innerHeight-.5)*2;
    rawMX=cx-rect.left; rawMY=cy-rect.top;
    if(!isMobile){glowEl.style.left=rawMX+'px';glowEl.style.top=rawMY+'px';}
  }
  if(!isMobile){ window.addEventListener('mousemove',onMouse,{passive:true}); }
  else { window.addEventListener('deviceorientation',function(e){
    mouseX=Math.max(-1,Math.min(1,(e.gamma||0)/20));
    mouseY=Math.max(-1,Math.min(1,(e.beta ||0)/20));
  },{passive:true}); }

  window.addEventListener('scroll',function(){
    var hp=document.getElementById('page-home');
    if(!hp||!hp.classList.contains('active')) return;
    var pageH=Math.max(document.body.scrollHeight-window.innerHeight,1);
    targetCamZ=-(Math.min(window.scrollY/pageH,1))*TUNNEL;
  },{passive:true});

  // ── CSS PARALLAX + MAGNETIC ───────────────────────────────────────────────
  var PARALLAX=[
    {sel:'.hero-badge',    d:0.22,r:0.8},
    {sel:'.hero-title',    d:0.18,r:0.6},
    {sel:'.hero-subtitle', d:0.14,r:0.4},
    {sel:'.hero-roles',    d:0.11,r:0.3},
    {sel:'.hero-desc',     d:0.08,r:0.2},
    {sel:'.hero-actions',  d:0.05,r:0.1},
    {sel:'.guarantee-badge',d:0.04,r:0.0},
    {sel:'.home-web-teaser',d:0.03,r:0.0},
    {sel:'.teaser-btn',    d:0.02,r:0.0},
  ];

  function applyParallax(mx,my){
    if(isMobile||prefersRM) return;
    PARALLAX.forEach(function(p){
      var el=document.querySelector(p.sel); if(!el) return;
      var tx=mx*p.d*30, ty=my*p.d*18;
      var rx=my*p.r*.4,  ry=mx*p.r*.4;
      el.style.transform='translate('+tx+'px,'+ty+'px) rotateX('+rx+'deg) rotateY('+ry+'deg)';
    });
  }

  function magneticCTA(mx,my){
    if(isMobile||prefersRM) return;
    document.querySelectorAll('.btn-primary,.btn-ghost').forEach(function(btn){
      var rect=btn.getBoundingClientRect();
      var bx=(rect.left+rect.right)/2/window.innerWidth*2-1;
      var by=(rect.top +rect.bottom)/2/window.innerHeight*2-1;
      var dx=mx-bx, dy=my-by;
      var dist=Math.sqrt(dx*dx+dy*dy);
      btn.style.transform=(dist<0.35)?
        'translate('+(dx*(0.35-dist)/0.35*10)+'px,'+(dy*(0.35-dist)/0.35*6)+'px)':'';
    });
  }

  function updateFrames(camZv){
    var min=999, idx=-1;
    frameObjects.forEach(function(f,i){var d=Math.abs(camZv-f.z);if(d<min){min=d;idx=i;}});
    frameObjects.forEach(function(f,i){
      var tgt=(i===idx&&min<14)?Math.min(f.base*2.4,1):f.base;
      f.mat.opacity+=(tgt-f.mat.opacity)*0.08;
    });
    frameLabel.style.opacity=(min<10)?'0.6':'0';
  }

  // ── RENDER LOOP ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  var clock=0;
  function animate(){
    requestAnimationFrame(animate);
    clock+=0.007;
    var hp=document.getElementById('page-home');
    if(!hp||!hp.classList.contains('active')) return;

    // Intro zoom + scroll
    camZ+=(targetCamZ-camZ)*(camZ>5?0.025:0.038);
    camera.position.z=camZ;

    smoothMX+=(mouseX-smoothMX)*0.07;
    smoothMY+=(mouseY-smoothMY)*0.07;
    camera.rotation.y= smoothMX*0.07;
    camera.rotation.x=-smoothMY*0.045;
    camera.position.y=Math.sin(clock*0.6)*0.05;

    orbMesh.position.x+=(smoothMX*3.5-orbMesh.position.x)*0.08;
    orbMesh.position.y+=(-smoothMY*2.0-orbMesh.position.y)*0.08;
    haloMesh.rotation.z=clock*0.9;

    var moving=Math.abs(mouseX-lastMouseX)>0.001;
    if(moving){orbOpacity=Math.min(orbOpacity+0.04,0.38);idleTimer=0;}
    else{idleTimer++;if(idleTimer>90)orbOpacity=Math.max(orbOpacity-0.008,0);}
    lastMouseX=mouseX;
    orbMesh.material.opacity =orbOpacity;
    haloMesh.material.opacity=orbOpacity*0.65;
    glowEl.style.opacity=(orbOpacity*2.5).toString();

    orbitals.forEach(function(o){
      o.rotation.x+=0.008*o._spd; o.rotation.y+=0.012*o._spd;
      o.position.y=o._origY+Math.sin(clock*o._spd+o._origY)*0.25;
    });

    applyParallax(smoothMX,smoothMY);
    magneticCTA(mouseX,mouseY);
    updateFrames(camZ);
    frameGroup.rotation.z=Math.sin(clock*0.35)*0.006;
    renderer.render(scene,camera);
  }
  animate();

  window.addEventListener('resize',function(){
    var w=container.offsetWidth||window.innerWidth;
    var h=container.offsetHeight||window.innerHeight;
    camera.aspect=w/h; camera.fov=window.innerWidth<768?65:55;
    camera.updateProjectionMatrix(); renderer.setSize(w,h);
  });

  window.resetHeroCamera = function(){
    targetCamZ = 0;
    camZ = 0;
  };

})(); // end initHero3D

// Section-tag underline on scroll
const tagObserver = new IntersectionObserver(entries=>{
  entries.forEach(e=>{ if(e.isIntersecting) e.target.classList.add('visible'); });
},{threshold:0.8});
document.querySelectorAll('.section-tag').forEach(el=>tagObserver.observe(el));

// Testimonial slide-in
const testiCards = document.querySelectorAll('.testi-card');
testiCards.forEach((card,i)=>{ card.classList.add(i%2===0?'hidden-left':'hidden-right'); });
const testiObserver = new IntersectionObserver(entries=>{
  entries.forEach(e=>{
    if(e.isIntersecting){
      e.target.classList.remove('hidden-left','hidden-right');
      testiObserver.unobserve(e.target);
    }
  });
},{threshold:0.15});
testiCards.forEach(c=>testiObserver.observe(c));

// Magnetic buttons
document.querySelectorAll('.btn-primary,.btn-ghost').forEach(btn=>{
  btn.addEventListener('mousemove',e=>{
    const r=btn.getBoundingClientRect();
    const dx=(e.clientX-r.left-r.width/2)*.2;
    const dy=(e.clientY-r.top-r.height/2)*.2;
    btn.style.transform=`translate(${dx}px,${dy}px) translateY(-1px)`;
  });
  btn.addEventListener('mouseleave',()=>{ btn.style.transform=''; });
});

// Counter animation on proof numbers
function animateCount(el, target, suffix=''){
  let start=0, dur=1600;
  const isFloat = target%1!==0;
  const step = timestamp=>{
    if(!start) start=timestamp;
    const p=Math.min((timestamp-start)/dur,1);
    const ease=1-Math.pow(1-p,3);
    const val = isFloat ? (ease*target).toFixed(1) : Math.floor(ease*target);
    el.textContent = val + suffix;
    if(p<1) requestAnimationFrame(step);
  };
  requestAnimationFrame(step);
}
const proofObserver = new IntersectionObserver(entries=>{
  entries.forEach(e=>{
    if(e.isIntersecting){
      const nums = e.target.querySelectorAll('.proof-num');
      nums.forEach(n=>{
        const raw = n.textContent;
        if(raw.includes('%')){ animateCount(n,parseFloat(raw),'%'); }
        else if(raw.includes('★')){ n.textContent=raw; }
        else if(raw.includes('+')){animateCount(n,parseInt(raw),'+');}
        else if(raw.includes('H')){ n.textContent=raw; }
        else if(raw.includes('11')){ animateCount(n,11,'%'); }
      });
      proofObserver.unobserve(e.target);
    }
  });
},{threshold:0.5});
const proofEl = document.querySelector('.hero-proof');
if(proofEl) proofObserver.observe(proofEl);

